hi there steve kaufman here  uh today i'm going to talk about  polyglottary and  multilingualism  polyglottary is a term that i became  aware of only fairly recently it's a  term coined i think by doctor or by  professor alex argelis  and  he has  dedicated his life to learning languages  and he's learned quite a range of  languages  probably languages in all the different  language families and modern languages  ancient languages and he has a very  disciplined approach to language  learning and has a lot of experience and  it's his idea to set up an institute  where he would teach people how to learn  multiple languages how to become  polyglots and it's his idea to invite  a number of accomplished polyglots to  work with him  and to share their own experiences and  he he in fact invites people with  different  approaches to language learning to share  their experience  which i think is a very good idea  because  there are a lot of polyglots i call them  linguists people who speak a lot of  languages  uh  on the internet for example but not only  on the internet there's a number of them  everywhere  but the ones that we can listen to on  youtube  they have a number of things  approaches and opinions on how to learn  etc which they share which which are  common to them but they all have things  that they do differently and so to have  these polyglots come in and  you know expose potential language  learnings to language learners to  different approaches i think is a very  good thing because if these proficient  polyglots can have different approaches  that would suggest that that learners  would benefit from at least  looking at different possible approaches  and then deciding which one works best  for them  uh i must say that in my own experience  you know i never really approached  language learning as sort of trying to  learn as many languages as i could or or  even you know  prior to say  the most recent 10 years i would learn a  language that i needed to learn because  i was there i needed to learn french  when i was studying in france  uh i was sent to hong kong to learn  chinese i lived in japan i got  interested in german we were doing  business in germany  spanish swedish it was always  a specific interest in a specific  language without necessarily thinking  when i finish this language then i'll do  another language whereas today i i must  admit that i'm my mood now is that  if i if i get to a  sufficient level in my check  then i'm going to do another language i  don't know whether i will you know stay  in the slavic area and go on to do  polish or whether i'll go off into the  middle east and do turkish or farsi or  arabic  uh but i definitely want to learn  another language so you know i've been  bitten by the polyglot bug  uh however you know in all of this  what strikes me is there are lots of  these keener polyglots i don't know if  there are more  today than in previous times but i think  we are more aware of them because of  youtube and the internet  uh for example at link i'm amazed to see  the number of people on our site who  study multiple languages three four five  six eight languages that they're  studying at the same time  and i always thought that people would  come on to study one language somebody  wanted to learn spanish would come on  and learn spanish or people who were  needed to improve their english or the  french or the russian or the chinese but  actually people are studying five six  languages so i think this is an  interesting trend  um typically in school we have one maybe  two languages  and typically in north america it's one  language in europe it might be two but  it would be two european languages but  what we're now seeing via the internet  and elsewhere is that people are  studying asian languages african  languages middle eastern languages  people are really getting into this  whole polyglot  uh field of activity and so i think in  that sense professor argelia's  initiative could be a very interesting  one because i think there are a lot of  people who would like to learn multiple  languages and  all i am sure all the polyglots will  tell you that with each  uh  you know successive language it gets  easier to learn plus you can group them  you know if i do russian i can do check  and then i can do polish  and certainly the chinese helps because  of the vocabulary with your japanese and  then you can take your chinese and  japanese that'll help you with your  korean  so you can start knocking off some of  these languages and build up your uh  real build up your resume if you're so  inclined  but  with all of that though i still come  back to the fact that a majority of  people particularly in the  english-speaking world  only speak one language and of course  that's because english is such a  dominant international language which is  a good thing i sometimes see people  knocking english when i see  you know  people from foreign countries  foreign cultures  uh communicating with each other  so well in english i realized what a  tremendously  useful and practical tool  worldwide english is  but  it does make it easy for english  speakers to be lazy  and yet i constantly come across people  say even here in vancouver when i tell  them that i have this website for  language like oh i'm really interested  i've always wanted to learn spanish i  always wanted to learn chinese or french  so at that level these people are very  interested uh  and many of them go take courses  but how many of them really follow  through  and so so it's one thing to you know  professor argelis is going to help  people have five languages and move it  up to ten  but  how do we get the people who only speak  one language and it's not just english  speakers let's be fair i was at a at a  charity concert the other evening  you know  on behalf of the victims  of the tsunami in japan who are still  dealing with lots and lots of problems  and the majority of people at this  charity concert were people from the  japanese community here in vancouver and  particularly first generation immigrants  people who have been living here for 20  30 years and i was quite astounded by  how poor the average level of english  was amongst these people  and so i'm always  you know i come back to this point how  do you help people learn that first  foreign language well because that's the  biggest step what can we do can we  spread the word can we  you know convince people that that they  need to spend the time the need to get  motivated to learn the language they  don't need to get hung up on the grammar  up front uh again this you know this  russian fellow at the university of  dalana saying you know if you want to  learn language go out and buy a book on  linguistics most people aren't going to  do that most people are i still believe  somewhat intimidated by all these rules  upfront and frustrated that they can't  remember them and frustrated that they  can't produce the language effectively  and and it's my belief as many of you  who have seen me here before  will know that if you can get people to  relax enjoy the language find a way to  enjoy the language enjoy the songs enjoy  the tv programs read listen don't worry  about speaking until you want to or have  an opportunity to can we make that first  language easier can we somehow encourage  people to expose themselves to more of  the language but in a way where they  actually start to notice like i was  quite impressed by these  many of these japanese canadians been  here 20 30 years  who still use japanese sentence  or word order  and and and yet and yet you'll see young  people who come here from japan  on a working holiday and they work in uh  in a restaurant where they're serving  uh you know canadians uh  and they're speaking english all the  time and their their attitude they're  they're fun they want to be with it they  want to be part of the community and  those people learn to speak very quickly  so  you know i don't know  really how we get this sense of just  have fun with the language  out to more people so that  we can genuinely get more people  speaking more than one language because  if they get the first one down then they  can get the second one and from then on  you know it's not a big big step to  becoming a polyglot  but you first have to get that first  language down so i'd be interested in  knowing what people out there think can  be done to help the many many people who  struggle with learning that first  foreign language once we do that we can  move on to polyglottery thank you for  listening

